/// `layout-as-model` states the layout of a truth value in one bit. The
/// layout is a model of the theory of truth.
///
/// Implemented: the layout is a module that models the theory of its type.
/// Its carrier is the terms of a theory of one bit. The source holds the two
/// folds, an equivalence, and its path by `ua`. The kernel replays the two
/// round trips of the equivalence.
///
/// Not implemented: the tree derives no fold into a model. It has no fold out
/// of the terms into a model value.
///
/// The author writes each fold by hand. The bits are terms, and no memory
/// holds one.
///
/// - owes: #4148 — the derived fold into a model module
/// - owes: #4149 — the fold out of the terms into a model value
/// - see: corpus/model/layouts/truth-in-one-bit.gandr

// `Truth`, `Bit`, `encode`, `decode` and `bitPath`.
import "../../model/layouts/truth-in-one-bit.gandr";

/// The layout stores the true value as the constant one.
@[runs("Bit.one")]
def laidOut() -> Terms Bit = encode Truth.yes;

/// A bit term whose rule fires reads back as the true value.
@[runs("Truth.yes")]
def readBack() -> Terms Truth = decode (Bit.band Bit.one (encode Truth.yes));

/// A truth value moves to its bit along the path between the two types.
@[runs("Bit.one")]
def moved() -> Terms Bit = Path.walk (fun (X) { X }) bitPath Truth.yes;
